WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.470
When designing a layer for

2
00:00:01.470 --> 00:00:03.400
a conv layer, you
might have to pick.

3
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.500
Do you want a
one-by-three filter

4
00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:07.390
or three-by-three
or five-by-five,

5
00:00:07.390 --> 00:00:09.160
or do you want a polling layer?

6
00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:10.980
What the exception network

7
00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:13.260
does is it says, why
don't you do them all?

8
00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:16.240
This makes the network
architecture more complicated,

9
00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:18.360
but it also works
remarkably well.

10
00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:19.760
Let's see how this works.

11
00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:22.655
Let's say for the second
example that you have

12
00:00:22.655 --> 00:00:27.280
input a 28 by 28 by
192-dimensional volume.

13
00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:30.120
What the inception network

14
00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:32.500
or what an inception
layer says is,

15
00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:34.820
instead of choosing
what filter size

16
00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:36.700
you want in a conv layer

17
00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:38.160
or even do you want

18
00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:39.380
a convolutional layer or

19
00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:41.770
polling layer,
let's do them all.

20
00:00:41.770 --> 00:00:46.860
What if you can use a
one-by-one convolution,

21
00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:50.745
and that will output a
28 by 28 by something,

22
00:00:50.745 --> 00:00:56.160
let's say, 28 by
28 by 64 output,

23
00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:58.455
and you just have
a volume there?

24
00:00:58.455 --> 00:01:01.700
But maybe you also want
to try a three by three,

25
00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:06.545
and that might output
a 20 by 28 by 128.

26
00:01:06.545 --> 00:01:09.255
Then what you do is just

27
00:01:09.255 --> 00:01:14.185
stack up this second volume
next to the first volume.

28
00:01:14.185 --> 00:01:16.450
To make the dimensions match up,

29
00:01:16.450 --> 00:01:19.530
let's make this
same convolution,

30
00:01:19.530 --> 00:01:23.480
so the output dimension
is still 28 by 28,

31
00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:26.965
same as the input dimension
in terms of height and width,

32
00:01:26.965 --> 00:01:31.425
but 28 by 28 but in
this example 128.

33
00:01:31.425 --> 00:01:33.320
Maybe you might say, well,

34
00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:34.540
I want to hedge my bets.

35
00:01:34.540 --> 00:01:36.055
Maybe a five-by-five filter

36
00:01:36.055 --> 00:01:38.140
works better so
let's do that too,

37
00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:44.850
and have that output
a 28 by 28 by 32.

38
00:01:44.850 --> 00:01:47.400
Again, you use the
same convolution

39
00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:49.590
to keep the dimensions same.

40
00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:52.700
Maybe you don't want
convolutional layer.

41
00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:56.420
Let's apply pulling, and
that has some other output,

42
00:01:56.420 --> 00:01:58.895
and let's stack that up as well.

43
00:01:58.895 --> 00:02:05.150
Here, pulling outputs
28 by 28 by 32.

44
00:02:05.150 --> 00:02:09.170
Now, in order to make all
the dimensions match,

45
00:02:09.170 --> 00:02:12.670
you actually need to use
padding for Max pooling.

46
00:02:12.670 --> 00:02:15.870
This is an unusual form of
polling because if you want

47
00:02:15.870 --> 00:02:17.770
the input to have
height and worth 28

48
00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:20.270
by 28 and have the output,

49
00:02:20.270 --> 00:02:24.430
match the dimension and
everything else also by 28 by 28,

50
00:02:24.430 --> 00:02:25.990
then you need to use

51
00:02:25.990 --> 00:02:31.205
same padding as well as a
stride of one for polling.

52
00:02:31.205 --> 00:02:34.210
This detail might seem
a bit funny to you now,

53
00:02:34.210 --> 00:02:36.465
but let's keep going.

54
00:02:36.465 --> 00:02:39.410
We'll make this all work later.

55
00:02:39.410 --> 00:02:43.030
But with a inception
module like this,

56
00:02:43.030 --> 00:02:45.190
you can input some volume

57
00:02:45.190 --> 00:02:47.030
and output, in this
case, I guess,

58
00:02:47.030 --> 00:02:48.650
if you add up all these numbers,

59
00:02:48.650 --> 00:02:55.150
32 plus 32 plus 128 plus
64 that's equal to 256.

60
00:02:55.150 --> 00:03:01.550
You will have one inception
module input 28 by 28 by 192,

61
00:03:01.550 --> 00:03:06.420
and output 28 by 28 by 256.

62
00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:10.670
This is the heart of
the inception network,

63
00:03:10.670 --> 00:03:12.970
which is due to Cristian Zagoi,

64
00:03:12.970 --> 00:03:14.270
We Lu Yantin Zia,

65
00:03:14.270 --> 00:03:16.410
Pi, Scott Reed, Dragoma,

66
00:03:16.410 --> 00:03:20.830
Anglov Dmitri Rhon Vincent
VanKok, and Andrew Rabinovich.

67
00:03:20.830 --> 00:03:24.790
The basic idea is that
instead of you needing to

68
00:03:24.790 --> 00:03:27.270
pick one of these filter sizes

69
00:03:27.270 --> 00:03:29.890
or pulling you want and
committing to that,

70
00:03:29.890 --> 00:03:32.010
you can do them all
and just contact

71
00:03:32.010 --> 00:03:34.690
all the outputs and
let the network learn

72
00:03:34.690 --> 00:03:36.330
whatever parameters
it wants to use

73
00:03:36.330 --> 00:03:40.005
whatever combinations of
these filter sizes it wants.

74
00:03:40.005 --> 00:03:41.880
Now, it turns out that there's

75
00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:43.440
a problem with the
inception there,

76
00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:44.800
as we've described it here,

77
00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:46.800
which is computational cost.

78
00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:48.680
On the next slide, let's figure

79
00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:50.880
out what's the
computational cost of

80
00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:54.460
this five-by-five
filter resulting

81
00:03:54.460 --> 00:03:56.880
in this block over here.

82
00:03:56.880 --> 00:04:00.700
Just focusing on the
five by five part

83
00:04:00.700 --> 00:04:02.720
on the previous slide,

84
00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:06.980
we had this input a 288
by 28 by 192 block,

85
00:04:06.980 --> 00:04:10.100
and you implement a five
by five same convolution

86
00:04:10.100 --> 00:04:14.655
with 32 filters output
28 by 28 by 32.

87
00:04:14.655 --> 00:04:16.510
On the previous slide,

88
00:04:16.510 --> 00:04:18.750
I had drawn this as
a thin purple slide,

89
00:04:18.750 --> 00:04:20.010
but I'm just I draw this as

90
00:04:20.010 --> 00:04:23.350
a more normal-looking
blue block here.

91
00:04:23.350 --> 00:04:26.470
Let's look at the
computational cost

92
00:04:26.470 --> 00:04:31.070
of outputting this
20 by 20 by 32.

93
00:04:31.070 --> 00:04:37.990
You have 32 filters because
the output has 32 channels,

94
00:04:37.990 --> 00:04:44.460
and each filter is going
to be five by five by 192.

95
00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:48.370
The output size is
20 by 20 by 32,

96
00:04:48.370 --> 00:04:53.530
and so you need to compute
28 by 28 by 32 numbers,

97
00:04:53.530 --> 00:04:54.930
and for each of them,

98
00:04:54.930 --> 00:04:58.130
you need to do this
many multiplications,

99
00:04:58.130 --> 00:05:01.310
five by five by 192.

100
00:05:01.310 --> 00:05:03.650
The total number of
multipliers you need

101
00:05:03.650 --> 00:05:06.050
is the number of
multipliers you need

102
00:05:06.050 --> 00:05:08.630
to compute each of
the output values

103
00:05:08.630 --> 00:05:12.690
times the number of output
values you need to compute.

104
00:05:12.690 --> 00:05:15.250
If you multiply out
all these numbers,

105
00:05:15.250 --> 00:05:19.430
this is equal to 120 million.

106
00:05:19.870 --> 00:05:22.870
While you can do 120 million

107
00:05:22.870 --> 00:05:24.670
multipliers on a
modern computer,

108
00:05:24.670 --> 00:05:27.370
this is still a pretty
expensive operation.

109
00:05:27.370 --> 00:05:29.430
On the next slide, you see how

110
00:05:29.430 --> 00:05:32.290
using the idea of
one-by-one convolutions,

111
00:05:32.290 --> 00:05:34.250
which you learned about
in the previous video,

112
00:05:34.250 --> 00:05:36.870
you'll be able to reduce
the computational cost

113
00:05:36.870 --> 00:05:38.870
by about a factor of 10 to

114
00:05:38.870 --> 00:05:41.910
go from about 120
million multipliers

115
00:05:41.910 --> 00:05:44.565
to about one tenth of that.

116
00:05:44.565 --> 00:05:47.450
Please remember the number 120,

117
00:05:47.450 --> 00:05:49.230
so you can compare it with what

118
00:05:49.230 --> 00:05:52.130
you see on the next slide, 128.

119
00:05:52.130 --> 00:05:55.370
Here's an alternative
architecture

120
00:05:55.370 --> 00:05:57.720
for inputting
twenty-eight by 20 by

121
00:05:57.720 --> 00:06:03.070
192 and outputting 28 by 20
by 32 which is following.

122
00:06:03.070 --> 00:06:05.170
You're going to
input the volume,

123
00:06:05.170 --> 00:06:07.790
use a one-by-one convolution,

124
00:06:07.790 --> 00:06:10.170
to reduce the volume to

125
00:06:10.170 --> 00:06:14.570
a 16 channels instead
of 192 channels,

126
00:06:14.570 --> 00:06:17.390
and then on this
much smaller volume,

127
00:06:17.390 --> 00:06:19.550
run your five-by-five
convolution

128
00:06:19.550 --> 00:06:22.050
to give you your final output.

129
00:06:22.050 --> 00:06:23.430
Notice, the input and output

130
00:06:23.430 --> 00:06:24.750
dimensions are still the same.

131
00:06:24.750 --> 00:06:31.210
You input 28 by 20 by 182
and output 28 by 28 by two,

132
00:06:31.210 --> 00:06:33.345
same as the previous line.

133
00:06:33.345 --> 00:06:35.220
But what we've done
is we've taken

134
00:06:35.220 --> 00:06:37.360
this huge volume we
had on the left,

135
00:06:37.360 --> 00:06:38.900
and we've shrunk it to

136
00:06:38.900 --> 00:06:42.180
this much smaller
intermediate volume,

137
00:06:42.180 --> 00:06:46.680
which is only has 16
instead of 192 channels.

138
00:06:46.680 --> 00:06:52.060
Sometimes this is called
a bottleneck layer.

139
00:06:54.560 --> 00:06:57.980
I guess because a bottleneck

140
00:06:57.980 --> 00:06:59.890
is usually the smallest
part of something.

141
00:06:59.890 --> 00:07:04.670
I guess if you have a glass
bottle that looks like this,

142
00:07:04.670 --> 00:07:08.960
then this is I guess
where the core goes.

143
00:07:08.960 --> 00:07:13.750
Then the bottleneck is the
smallest part of this bottle.

144
00:07:13.750 --> 00:07:16.150
In the same way, the
bottleneck layer

145
00:07:16.150 --> 00:07:18.010
is the smallest part
of this network,

146
00:07:18.010 --> 00:07:19.450
where you strength
the representation

147
00:07:19.450 --> 00:07:22.660
before increasing
the size again.

148
00:07:22.660 --> 00:07:26.950
Now, let's look at the
computational costs involved.

149
00:07:26.950 --> 00:07:30.330
To apply this
one-by-one convolution,

150
00:07:30.330 --> 00:07:32.410
we have 16 filters.

151
00:07:32.410 --> 00:07:34.490
Each of the filters
is going to be

152
00:07:34.490 --> 00:07:37.170
of dimension one by one by 192.

153
00:07:37.170 --> 00:07:40.410
This 192 matches that 192.

154
00:07:40.410 --> 00:07:42.790
The cost of computing this 28

155
00:07:42.790 --> 00:07:45.290
by 28 by 16 volume
is going to be,

156
00:07:45.290 --> 00:07:48.205
well, you need
this many outputs.

157
00:07:48.205 --> 00:07:54.900
For each of them, you need
to do 192 multiplications.

158
00:07:54.900 --> 00:07:59.460
I could have written one
times one times 192, is this?

159
00:07:59.460 --> 00:08:00.920
If you multiply this out,

160
00:08:00.920 --> 00:08:02.660
this is 2.4 million.

161
00:08:02.660 --> 00:08:04.360
It is about 2.4 million.

162
00:08:04.360 --> 00:08:06.780
How about the second?
That's the cost

163
00:08:06.780 --> 00:08:11.440
of this first
convolutional layer.

164
00:08:11.440 --> 00:08:15.200
The cost of this second
convolutional layer will be that.

165
00:08:15.200 --> 00:08:17.340
Well, you have
this many outputs,

166
00:08:17.340 --> 00:08:20.395
28 by 28 by two.

167
00:08:20.395 --> 00:08:22.920
Then for each of the outputs,

168
00:08:22.920 --> 00:08:24.565
you have to apply a

169
00:08:24.565 --> 00:08:28.460
five-by-five by
16-dimensional filter.

170
00:08:28.460 --> 00:08:31.280
Five by five by 16,

171
00:08:31.280 --> 00:08:36.695
and if we multiply that
out is equal to 10.0.

172
00:08:36.695 --> 00:08:38.880
The total number of
multiplications you

173
00:08:38.880 --> 00:08:41.020
need to do is the sum of those,

174
00:08:41.020 --> 00:08:45.090
which is 12.4 million
multiplications.

175
00:08:45.090 --> 00:08:46.400
If you compare this with what

176
00:08:46.400 --> 00:08:47.940
we had on the previous slide,

177
00:08:47.940 --> 00:08:49.840
you reduce the
computational cost

178
00:08:49.840 --> 00:08:53.100
from about 120
million multipliers

179
00:08:53.100 --> 00:08:55.620
down to about one-tenth of that

180
00:08:55.620 --> 00:08:59.430
to 12.4 million multiplications.

181
00:08:59.430 --> 00:09:02.340
The number of additions
you need to do is

182
00:09:02.340 --> 00:09:04.380
about very similar to

183
00:09:04.380 --> 00:09:06.300
the number of multiplications
you need to do,

184
00:09:06.300 --> 00:09:07.980
so that's why I'm just counting

185
00:09:07.980 --> 00:09:10.360
the number of multiplications.

186
00:09:10.360 --> 00:09:13.540
To summarize, if you're
building a layer of

187
00:09:13.540 --> 00:09:16.160
a neural network and you
don't want to have to decide,

188
00:09:16.160 --> 00:09:17.860
do you want a one by one or

189
00:09:17.860 --> 00:09:20.140
three by three or five by
five, or pulling layer,

190
00:09:20.140 --> 00:09:22.320
the inception module
lets you say,

191
00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:25.700
let's do them all and
let's concate the results.

192
00:09:25.700 --> 00:09:27.420
Then we ran to the problem

193
00:09:27.420 --> 00:09:29.080
of computational
cost and which you

194
00:09:29.080 --> 00:09:32.480
saw here was how using a
one-by-one convolution,

195
00:09:32.480 --> 00:09:34.660
you can create this
bottleneck layer

196
00:09:34.660 --> 00:09:38.100
thereby reducing the
computational cost significantly.

197
00:09:38.100 --> 00:09:39.740
Now, you might be wondering,

198
00:09:39.740 --> 00:09:41.200
does shrinking down

199
00:09:41.200 --> 00:09:43.380
the representation
size so dramatically,

200
00:09:43.380 --> 00:09:46.740
does it hurt the performance
of your neural network?

201
00:09:46.740 --> 00:09:48.900
It turns out that so long as you

202
00:09:48.900 --> 00:09:52.460
implement this bottleneck
layer so that within reason,

203
00:09:52.460 --> 00:09:54.200
you can shrink down

204
00:09:54.200 --> 00:09:56.440
the representation
size significantly,

205
00:09:56.440 --> 00:09:59.160
and it doesn't seem to
hurt the performance,

206
00:09:59.160 --> 00:10:01.740
but saves you a lot
of computation.

207
00:10:01.740 --> 00:10:04.880
So this is the key of these

208
00:10:04.880 --> 00:10:07.880
are the key ideas of
the inception module.

209
00:10:07.880 --> 00:10:10.920
Let's put them together
and in the next video show

210
00:10:10.920 --> 00:10:15.370
you what the full inception
network looks like.